<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Deliberately minimal accessibility service configuration.

    accessibilityEventTypes="typeViewTextChanged|typeViewLongClicked|typeViewSelected|typeWindowContentChanged"
        Three PERMANENT event types, plus one TEMPORARY DIAGNOSTIC (v3,
        see its own paragraph below).

        typeViewTextChanged powers the typed "?en"/"?pl" trigger pathway:
        "the text in the field currently being edited changed."

        typeViewLongClicked powers the separate long-press "translate what's
        under my finger" bubble pathway, added later: "the user long-pressed
        a view." This is the standard, purpose-built accessibility signal
        for a long-press and requires no additional capability flag to
        receive — in particular it does NOT require
        flagRequestTouchExplorationMode, which would turn the whole device
        into TalkBack-style screen-reader navigation and was deliberately
        rejected as a way to detect this gesture. One direct consequence of
        using this event instead of raw touch tracking: this service cannot
        observe exactly when the user's finger lifts off the screen, only
        that a long-press happened — which is why the resulting translation
        bubble (see TranslationBubble.kt) dismisses itself via an explicit
        close button, a tap outside the bubble, or an auto-dismiss timer,
        rather than "on finger release."

        typeViewSelected was added after real-device testing showed some
        apps' message lists (confirmed: the stock/Google Messages SMS app)
        never dispatch typeViewLongClicked at all for a long-press —
        unlike WhatsApp and Telegram, which do. The hypothesis was that such
        apps implement "long-press to enter multi-select mode" via their own
        touch/gesture handling rather than View.performLongClick(), the
        standard path that dispatches typeViewLongClicked, but that entering
        that selection state would still mark the selected row as
        isSelected and be announced via typeViewSelected. Real-device
        testing (v1.2.4) showed this event ALSO never fires for Google
        Messages — so this hypothesis, while still a reasonable one for
        OTHER apps, is now confirmed NOT to be the answer for Google
        Messages specifically. Left enabled because it is still a standard,
        zero-cost accessibility signal that may help other apps in the
        future, and disabling it fixes nothing.

        typeWindowContentChanged — TEMPORARY DIAGNOSTIC (v3). Research into
        why NEITHER of the two signals above fires for Google Messages
        turned up a more specific, documented explanation: Google Messages'
        message list is very likely built with Jetpack Compose, and
        Compose's combinedClickable(onLongClick = ...) handles a real
        physical long-press touch entirely inside Compose's own pointer-
        input system — it does not call View.performLongClick() at all, the
        classic dispatch path typeViewLongClicked depends on. Per Android's
        own Compose accessibility documentation (see README.md §14 "Second
        amendment to Feature update #6" for the exact source and quote),
        Compose instead exposes long-click and selection as
        AccessibilityNodeInfo actions/semantics properties, consumed by
        TalkBack calling performAction(ACTION_LONG_CLICK) itself — a
        service-INITIATED action, not a real user gesture, and not
        something this service (which never issues actions on other apps'
        nodes) can observe. If Compose's semantics for the RESULTING
        selection state change (a toolbar label changing, a checkmark
        appearing) are wired up correctly, its accessibility bridge should
        still emit typeWindowContentChanged for that resulting UI change,
        even though it emits nothing for the gesture itself. This temporary
        subscription exists ONLY to test that theory. It is handled by
        TextGateAccessibilityService.handleContentChangedDiagnostic(),
        which never reads node.text, never starts a translation, and shows
        only abstract, non-sensitive diagnostic data (package name, view
        class name, and the raw contentChangeTypes bitmask) — batched
        behind a debounce, since this event type fires very frequently for
        ordinary UI updates and would otherwise flood the screen. Remove
        this event type, its handler, and this paragraph once the
        investigation concludes either way.

        This service still does NOT receive typeViewFocused,
        typeWindowStateChanged, typeViewClicked (a normal, non-long tap),
        or any other event type — it still never observes navigation or
        which screens the user visits, only "a field's text changed," "a
        view was long-pressed," "a view became selected," and (temporarily,
        for diagnosis only) "a window's content changed."

    We deliberately do NOT set:
      - android:flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows — this service never enumerates
        or inspects any window other than the one that raised the event.
      - android:flagIncludeNotImportantViews — decorative/non-input views are
        never read.
      - android:packageNames — left unset (all packages) at the OS
        subscription level ONLY because the OS accessibility API has no
        concept of a *dynamically user-editable* allow-list here; the real
        enforcement happens first thing in
        TextGateAccessibilityService.onAccessibilityEvent(), which checks
        event.packageName against the user's saved allow-list and the
        built-in block-list and returns immediately for anything not
        explicitly allowed, before any further processing. This is true for
        ALL FOUR event types: the long-press bubble pathway (long-click AND
        selected) is gated by BubbleTranslateGate, which enforces the exact
        same block-list and allow-list as the typed-trigger pathway (see
        BubbleTranslateGate.kt); the temporary content-changed diagnostic
        applies the same master-switch/block-list/allow-list checks inline,
        before showing anything, even though it never reads message text.

    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true" is required for
    AccessibilityEvent#getSource() to return a usable node at all; without
    it the service could not read the field being edited, nor the
    long-pressed/selected message content, even after the user grants
    permission. This is intentionally the ONLY window-content capability
    granted — the service never calls AccessibilityService#getWindows() or
    #getRootInActiveWindow(), and never enumerates any window other than
    the one that raised the event; it only ever inspects event.getSource()
    and — for the long-press bubble pathway ONLY, when that node's own
    text is blank — that SAME node's own descendants (never siblings,
    never ancestors, bounded search; see BubbleTranslateGate.kt's
    "Where the text actually lives" doc section for the full reasoning).
    The typed-trigger pathway (EventGate) still only ever reads
    event.getSource() directly, with no further traversal of any kind. The
    temporary content-changed diagnostic reads only event.getSource()'s
    class name and screen bounds — never its text, never its children.

    android:notificationTimeout="0" — events are delivered immediately, not
    batched, so no accessibility event is ever buffered/queued by the
    framework on our behalf.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeViewTextChanged|typeViewLongClicked|typeViewSelected|typeWindowContentChanged"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags=""
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
    android:notificationTimeout="0"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_service_description"
    android:settingsActivity="com.textgate.ai.settings.SettingsActivity" />
